<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Retrieval-Augmented Generation systems often suffer from a gap between optimizing retrieval relevance and generative utility: retrieved documents may be topically relevant but still lack the content needed for effective reasoning during generation.
While existing ‘‘bridge’’ modules attempt to rewrite the retrieved text for better generation, we show how they fail to capture true document utility.
In this work, we propose R2U, with a key distinction of directly optimizing
to maximize the probability of generating a correct answer through process supervision.
As such direct observation is expensive, we also propose approximating an efficient distillation pipeline by scaling the supervision from LLMs, which helps the smaller rewriter model generalize better.
We evaluate our method across multiple open-domain question-answering benchmarks. The empirical results demonstrate consistent improvements over strong bridging baselines.
 <span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        Source code: <a href="https://anonymous.4open.science/r/R2U-D547/README.md" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://anonymous.4open.science/r/R2U-D547/README.md</a></span></span></span>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Despite the impressive capabilities of large language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Achiam et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2023)</a></cite>, they often struggle with answering queries unseen from training. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) offers an effective solution by using a retriever to gather unseen yet relevant documents and a generator to synthesize them into a final response.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15577v1/msmarco_ext_abs.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/237;" width="476" height="237"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Accuracy score gap of extractive vs abstractive answer types with different bridging models. For Naive RAG, the top-10 documents retrieved with Bing Search are passed to the generator. BGE-Reranker-Large <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al. (2023)</a></cite> is used for the rewriter baseline.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, a persistent gap remains between retrieval relevance and generative utility: improving retriever performance does not necessarily yield more accurate answers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ke et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
To bridge such a gap,
<span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bridge</span> modules between retriever (R) and generation (G) have been proposed, which rerank or summarize the retrieved documents to better support generation.
Examples include
reranking methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pradeep et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al. (2023)</a></cite> constrained to reorder documents without modifying their content, and query-focused summarization (QFS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yoon et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hwang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Edge et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite>
allowed to prune or enrich content selectively.
We abstract all these as <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">rewriting</span> of transformations from retrieved documents <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, considering <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> as well, towards optimizing <span id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">document utility</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our key claim is that existing rewriting modules fail to capture <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">true document utility, defined as its ability to support a generator for accurate answer generation</span>.
Existing work relies only on queries or retrieved documents as a proxy of such utility, but we argue that direct observation of answer generation is critical for capturing such utility.
To illustrate, one well-known bias of search engines
is ranking documents that lexically match queries high. Existing rewritings may improve extractive answers that can be directly taken from the documents, but may fail to help answer generation
requiring reasoning beyond direct extraction.
To demonstrate these limitations, we divided the MS MARCO dataset into extractive and abstractive answers,
depending on whether the ground truth answer
lexically matches within <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> or not.
As shown in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the well-known rewriting module, BGE-Reranker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al. (2023)</a></cite>,
boost extractive performance but harm performance in abstractive generation, revealing their limited generalization when deeper reasoning is required <span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          Other baseline results are in Table <a href="#A1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix A Details of Figure ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</span></span></span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As direct observation is costly, existing work observes
answer correctness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yoon et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hwang et al. (2024)</a></cite>, by rewriting first, then using answer correctness as a proxy observation. This risks spurious success, where the right answer is reached through flawed intermediate reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast,
we propose a novel bridging with direct observation, denoted as <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">r</span>elevance <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <span id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">u</span>tility (R2U).
We observe that sufficiently robust LLMs, during reasoning, can capture the desired process of how documents should be rewritten to support the answer.
With this process, we formally derive how the reasoning traces of robust LLMs approximate the ideal rewriter with true utility.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">However, such direct process observation is often
too expensive at inference time.
Therefore, we distill the direct observation of LLM,
into a smaller language model (SLM) as a rewriter model.
Beyond pushing inference cost to training time, this distillation enables more robust training of SLMs by scaling
observations from LLMs, for robustifying SLM supervision.
Specifically, in addition to observing
LLM’s rewriting processes for imitation,
we augment by reordering or omitting certain documents to help the SLM generalize better and mitigate positional bias.
We further train the SLM with preference learning, aligning its rewriting toward the generator’s performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our approach on multiple question-answering tasks covering diverse query types. The results show that R2U improves the average F1 score by 8.8%pt over the naive RAG baseline and outperforms the best existing baselines by 7.8%pt, consistently enhancing performance across tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Proposed Bridging Objective</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first formalize the role of bridge modules in RAG system, outlining where existing approaches fall short (§<a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Motivation: Existing Bridging Models ‣ 2 Proposed Bridging Objective ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). We then present our formulation of the bridging objective and how our objective captures true document utility. (§<a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Proposed: Process Supervision ‣ 2 Proposed Bridging Objective ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>–§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 R2U Training ‣ 3 Proposed Approach: R2U ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Motivation: Existing Bridging Models</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In RAG system, the generator <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> produces an answer <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> given query <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and retrieved documents <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\sim P_{\phi}(a|q,d)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\sim P_{\phi}(a|q,d)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Previous bridging works attempted to rewrite the documents to improve their utility,
typically by reordering the documents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pradeep et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al. (2023)</a></cite>, filtering out irrelevant contents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yoon et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite>, or reconstructing the inputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. (2025)</a></cite> using <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\sim P_{\phi}(a|q,d^{\prime}),~\textrm{where}~d^{\prime}\sim P_{\theta}(d^{\prime}|q,d)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\sim P_{\phi}(a|q,d^{\prime}),~\textrm{where}~d^{\prime}\sim P_{\theta}(d^{\prime}|q,d)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> denotes either a fine-tuned or off-the-shelf rewriting module, and <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the rewritten version of the documents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We argue that such bridges, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\theta}(d^{\prime}|q,d)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\theta}(d^{\prime}|q,d)</annotation></semantics></math>, cannot capture the true definition of document utility <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, which measures whether the rewritten document effectively supports the process of <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">answer generation</span> of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Proposed: Process Supervision</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The key objective of R2U is to learn a bridge model that rewrites documents to maximize their <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">true utility</span>, i.e., probability of generating a correct answer, formalized as <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="max_{d^{\prime}}P(a|q,d^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">max_{d^{\prime}}P(a|q,d^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, this notion of true utility is inherently unobservable. To optimize it directly, one would need an ideal rewriter that samples from <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\theta}(d^{\prime}|q,d,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\theta}(d^{\prime}|q,d,a)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., a rewriter that conditions on the target answer <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> during rewriting. Training a model with direct access to <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> constitutes a shortcut that the rewriter could simply encode the answer instead of genuinely learning to support answer generation. Thus, the challenge is how to approximate this ideal but unobservable distribution using practically observable signals, without shortcuts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our contribution is twofold. To address the above issue, we provide a derivation (§ <a href="#S2.SS2.SSS1" title="2.2.1 Formulation of R2U ‣ 2.2 Proposed: Process Supervision ‣ 2 Proposed Bridging Objective ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2.1</span></a>) that shows how the ideal rewrite distribution, otherwise unobservable, can be grounded in observable quantities obtained from LLM reasoning traces toward the answer.
A key prerequisite for this derivation is that rewritten documents
<math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> appear explicitly in these traces, allowing the distribution to be anchored in actual observations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, our second contribution is to demonstrate that sufficiently robust generators, e.g., 70B or larger, already satisfy the prerequisite for this derivation. During their internal reasoning process, they naturally perform the desired rewriting where the model produced rewrites <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> tailored to the target answer.
In our manual inspection, this behavior occured in 95% of cases, as shown in the examples in Table <a href="#A3.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Appendix C Details and Examples of LLM’s Reasoning Process ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> <span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            From chain-of-thought prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. (2022)</a></cite> 10 queries per model on Llama-3.1-{70b, 405b}-Instruct, claude-3-5-sonnet, Deepseek-r1, Mistral-{large,large2}, reka-flash, and snowflake-arctic.</span></span></span>.
This motivates using such LLMs to act simultaneously as both the rewriter and the answer generator, producing rewritten documents
<math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> during reasoning that are tailored to a specific answer <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">2.2.1 </span>Formulation of R2U</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section develops the first contribution, building on the prerequisite established above. We will show that what we observed from the LLM serves as the basis from which the ideal rewrite distribution can be derived.
With the ideal distribution, we obtain the document utility that directly aligns rewritten documents with answerability and avoids shortcuts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, what we <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">actually</span> observe from the teacher LLM’s reasoning traces are joint samples,</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(a,d^{\prime})\sim P_{\phi}(a,d^{\prime}\mid q,d)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,d^{\prime})\sim P_{\phi}(a,d^{\prime}\mid q,d),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the query and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is the retrieved documents.
This joint reflects the LLM’s process supervision as it generates <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> as part of its internal reasoning toward <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the chain rule, the joint can be factorized in two equivalent ways:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;P_{\phi}(a,d^{\prime}\mid q,d)\\
&amp;=P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d,a)\;P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d)\\
&amp;=P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime},d)\;P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d)\\
\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>P</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.560em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;P_{\phi}(a,d^{\prime}\mid q,d)\\
&amp;=P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d,a)\;P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d)\\
&amp;=P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime},d)\;P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d)\\
\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Under the natural <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sufficiency assumption</span> that once the rewritten documents <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are given from a sufficiently robust LLM, the original documents <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> barely provide information about <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
                
                
                
              We empirically validate that the performance difference with and without <math id="footnote4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is negligible, as shown in Table <a href="#A2.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix B Sufficiency Assumption ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</span></span></span>, which is formally
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime},d)\approx P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime},d)\approx P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, and thus:</p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="P_{\phi}(a,d^{\prime}\mid q,d)\approx P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime})\;P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\phi}(a,d^{\prime}\mid q,d)\approx P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime})\;P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">From this identity, the ideal rewrite distribution, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d,a)</annotation></semantics></math>, can be written as:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d,a)=\frac{P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime})\;P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d)}{P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d,a)=\frac{P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime})\;P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d)}{P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">which describes how the LLM rewrites documents when aiming to produce an answer <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, when we sample <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> from the LLM’s reasoning traces, we are implicitly sampling from a distribution close to
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d,a)</annotation></semantics></math>. Feeding such <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> back into the generator yields the bridge process:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a\sim P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime}),~\text{where}~d^{\prime}\sim P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d,a)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a\sim P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime}),~\text{where}~d^{\prime}\sim P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d,a)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We can now reformulate the bridging objective <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{d^{\prime}}P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{d^{\prime}}P_{\phi}(a\mid q,d^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> as an expectation over the ideal rewrite distribution:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}&amp;\textrm{maximize}~\mathbb{E}_{d^{\prime}\sim P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}|q,d,a)}\left[\log P_{\phi}(a|q,d^{\prime})\right]\\
&amp;\quad\quad=\mathbb{E}_{d^{\prime}\sim P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}|q,d)}\left[\frac{P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}|q,d,a)}{P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}|q,d)}\log P_{\phi}(a|q,d^{\prime})\right]\\
&amp;\left(\because\textrm{Importance-sampling identity}\right)\\
&amp;\quad\quad=\mathbb{E}_{d^{\prime}\sim P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}|q,d)}\left[\frac{P_{\phi}(a|q,d^{\prime})}{P_{\phi}(a|q,d)}\log P_{\phi}(a|q,d^{\prime})\right]\\
\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>maximize</mtext><mo lspace="0.660em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="2.278em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">∵</mo><mtext>Importance-sampling identity</mtext><mo>)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="2.278em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}&amp;\textrm{maximize}~\mathbb{E}_{d^{\prime}\sim P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}|q,d,a)}\left[\log P_{\phi}(a|q,d^{\prime})\right]\\
&amp;\quad\quad=\mathbb{E}_{d^{\prime}\sim P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}|q,d)}\left[\frac{P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}|q,d,a)}{P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}|q,d)}\log P_{\phi}(a|q,d^{\prime})\right]\\
&amp;\left(\because\textrm{Importance-sampling identity}\right)\\
&amp;\quad\quad=\mathbb{E}_{d^{\prime}\sim P_{\phi}(d^{\prime}|q,d)}\left[\frac{P_{\phi}(a|q,d^{\prime})}{P_{\phi}(a|q,d)}\log P_{\phi}(a|q,d^{\prime})\right]\\
\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The key insight emerges from the importance weight,
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{P_{\phi}(a|q,d^{\prime})}{P_{\phi}(a|q,d)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{P_{\phi}(a|q,d^{\prime})}{P_{\phi}(a|q,d)}</annotation></semantics></math>,
which has a clear interpretation: it measures the relative <span id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">answerability</span> of the rewritten documents, compared to the original.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike prior objectives that ignore the answer during rewriting, our formulation directly observes answerability.
The prior rewriting objective in Eq. (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 Motivation: Existing Bridging Models ‣ 2 Proposed Bridging Objective ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) relies solely on a query and documents without incorporating the target answer <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>.
While some works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yoon et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite> use answers as a supervision to post-hoc filter rewritten documents, they only treat <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> as a prior by adding <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(a)</annotation></semantics></math> to Eq. (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 Motivation: Existing Bridging Models ‣ 2 Proposed Bridging Objective ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) rather than explicitly aligning for answerability.
In contrast, our formulation confirms that rewritten documents <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, sampled from the LLM’s internal reasoning process, maximize answerability.
Therefore, sampling <math id="S2.SS2.SSS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> from the LLM approximates ideal rewritten documents for the generator, without introducing shortcut signals.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Proposed Approach: R2U</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the core design of R2U (§<a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Proposed: Process Supervision ‣ 2 Proposed Bridging Objective ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>–§<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 R2U Training ‣ 3 Proposed Approach: R2U ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>).
Using a large reasoning model (e.g., 70B parameters) as a bridge is a straightforward solution but comes with high computational cost, especially when the answer generator is much smaller (e.g., 3B).
To address this, we train a smaller model to imitate the rewriting behavior of a larger teacher model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.15577v1/ours_overview.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/129;" width="476" height="129" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Pipeline of R2U. Through the generated document distribution that captures the notion of true utility, we train the small rewriter model as a bridge.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Distilling Scaled Process Supervision</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To avoid high computational cost of direct observation, we train a smaller relevance-to-utility rewriter model (R2U) to imitate direct observation.
Based on the formulation in §<a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Proposed: Process Supervision ‣ 2 Proposed Bridging Objective ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, we collect <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(d,d^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(d,d^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> pairs to construct training dataset for the small rewriter, where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from the LLM’s reasoning traces.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Still, smaller models have a limitation in that they remain susceptible to positional bias, which requires caution when distilling rewritten documents.
When they imitate from <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{LLM}}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{LLM}}(d^{\prime}\mid q,d,a)</annotation></semantics></math>,
they may be hallucinated by under-emphasized documents in middle positions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, leading to overlooked content and lower distillation quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">inference scaling</span>
for expanding each training example into <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> separate LLM calls, as shown in the upper part of Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Proposed Approach: R2U ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
The key idea is to augment each document to mitigate bias, inspired by unbiased training from summarization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hwang et al. (2024)</a></cite> that rewrites each sentence individually based on the entire document.
Specifically, for each of the top-<math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> retrieved documents, we prompt the LLM to rewrite one target document of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\textrm{LLM}}(d_{i}^{\prime}\mid q,d,a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>LLM</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\textrm{LLM}}(d_{i}^{\prime}\mid q,d,a)</annotation></semantics></math> at a time, conditioned on the query and the remaining documents.
During this process, the target document is placed at the end of the context to mitigate positional bias as well. While this increases inference cost, this robust dataset generation is done only once offline.
Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.1 Distilling Scaled Process Supervision ‣ 3 Proposed Approach: R2U ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> repeats the experiment from Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, showing that both our scaled rewriting and its ablation outperform NaiveRAG, with our full method achieving a significantly larger gain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Then, we design a revised CoT prompt template to observe the desired document distribution from the reasoning process. A part of our prompt is described below.
To ensure better supervision quality, we have made several design choices:
(1) Separate the document rewriting step from the reasoning and answering step, and discard the latter step to prevent leakage. (2) Restrict rewrites to retrieved content. (3) Encourage cross-document interaction. (4) Allow skipping irrelevant documents.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_p">Think step by step to answer the query based solely on the target document, while using the other documents to help clarify and improve the target document. […] 
<br class="ltx_break">[Guidelines] 
<br class="ltx_break">If the document does not contain any content relevant to the query, return exactly: <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[NO_REWRITE]</span> […] 
<br class="ltx_break">Output format: 
<br class="ltx_break">Step 1. Document rewrite: <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">[Rewritten document]</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">Step 2. Explain and answer: <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[Explain and answer]</span></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15577v1/msmarco_abs_only2.svg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/280;" width="476" height="280"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Ablation study of ours on abstractive subset of MSMARCO. <span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct</span> is used as the bridge.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>R2U Training</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in the lower part of Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Proposed Approach: R2U ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we train an SLM with a standard language modeling objective to imitate the rewriting behavior of the teacher LLM. We begin by fine-tuning the SLM on the supervision dataset described in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Distilling Scaled Process Supervision ‣ 3 Proposed Approach: R2U ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>.
Then, as shown in the right part of Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Proposed Approach: R2U ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>,
we apply Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al. (2023)</a></cite> to let the SLM adapt its rewriting preferences.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Supervised Fine-Tuning</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">First, the SLM learns to generate the rewritten document set <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on the original retrieved documents <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and the query <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>.
Training is performed by minimizing the negative log-likelihood over the entire token sequence of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, generated in a single forward pass:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{SLM}}=-\log P_{\textrm{R2U }}(d^{\prime}_{1},...,d^{\prime}_{k}\mid q,\{d_{1},...,d_{k}\})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>SLM</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>R2U </mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{SLM}}=-\log P_{\textrm{R2U }}(d^{\prime}_{1},...,d^{\prime}_{k}\mid q,\{d_{1},...,d_{k}\})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}_{1},...,d^{\prime}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}_{1},...,d^{\prime}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the token sequence representing the complete set of rewritten documents, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{d_{1},...,d_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{d_{1},...,d_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the original retrieved documents.
Note that we concatenate all <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> augmentations for students to generate a single rewrite of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> documents in a single inference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Preference Optimization</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further optimize the SLM with DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al. (2023)</a></cite> to align its rewriting preferences with those that lead to correct answers. While the teacher model provides high-quality rewritings, these may not always align with what is optimal for a smaller generator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ke et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
DPO enables smaller models to adjust toward their own optimal preferences rather than strictly imitating the teacher.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The key challenge in DPO is that preference pairs must be constructed over <em id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sets</em> of rewritten documents, not <em id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">individual</em> documents.
A naive construction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al. (2024)</a></cite> would only collect individual documents that always yield correct answers (F1 = 1) and contrast with those that always fail (F1 = 0), but it severely underutilizes the available supervision.
The analysis in SubSec. <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 RQ 3: Does each component of R2U contribute to overall effectiveness? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a> shows that even when individual rewritten documents are partially informative in isolation, their combination can result in a correct answer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To overcome this challenge, we augment the preference pairs at the set level by leveraging documents with varying individual answer correctness.
Specifically, we begin by evaluating <em id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">individual</em> rewritten document, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, in isolation, categorizing it into one of three groups based on its F1 score: (a) F1 = 0, (b) <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;</annotation></semantics></math> F1 <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, and (c) F1 = 1. We then construct document <em id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sets</em>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, by combining these individual documents according to their categories—for example, sets containing only type (a), a mix of (a) and (b), a mix of (b) and (c), or other combinations including type (c). Each document set is then used as input to the model to determine whether it produces a correct answer. If the generated answer achieves F1 = 1, the document set is labeled as a positive example; else if F1 = 0, it is labeled as negative. These positive and negative document sets are used to construct win–lose pairs dataset <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> for DPO training:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}&amp;=-\mathbb{E}_{(d,d^{\prime}_{w},d^{\prime}_{l})\sim\mathcal{D}}\\
&amp;\left[\log\sigma\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(d^{\prime}_{w}|d)}{\pi_{\phi}(d^{\prime}_{w}|d)}-\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(d^{\prime}_{l}|d)}{\pi_{\phi}(d^{\prime}_{l}|d)}\right)\right]\\
\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DPO</mtext></msub></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\mathcal{L}_{\text{DPO}}&amp;=-\mathbb{E}_{(d,d^{\prime}_{w},d^{\prime}_{l})\sim\mathcal{D}}\\
&amp;\left[\log\sigma\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(d^{\prime}_{w}|d)}{\pi_{\phi}(d^{\prime}_{w}|d)}-\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(d^{\prime}_{l}|d)}{\pi_{\phi}(d^{\prime}_{l}|d)}\right)\right]\\
\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the policy model that needs to be optimized, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the original policy model with fixed parameters.
<math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is the training set, where the input of each data is <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and the output is a pair of rewritten document lists, with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d^{\prime}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>l</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d^{\prime}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> as the winner set and loser set, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:502.7pt;height:106.1pt;vertical-align:-50.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-37.6pt,7.9pt) scale(0.9,0.9) ;">
