<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Experimental Setting</h3>

<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model and Hyperparameters</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AI@Meta (2024)</a></cite> for synthetic data generation and train <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct</span> to build our R2U rewriter.
Following prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2023)</a></cite> that sample 10k–50k entries from the MS MARCO training set due to computational constraints, we randomly select a subset within this range, about 36k queries in the full training set.
We train the model using DeepSpeed ZeRO-3 with offloading <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rasley et al. (2020)</a></cite> on a cluster of 8 NVIDIA A6000 GPUs. We used VLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al. (2023)</a></cite> during inference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following evaluation conventions of rewriting works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chirkova et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yoon et al. (2024)</a></cite>, we evaluate R2U on question answering (QA) benchmarks.
We first evaluate on multi-hop QA datasets: <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HotpotQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2018)</a></cite>, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2WikiMultihopQA (2WIKI)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al. (2020)</a></cite>, and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MuSiQue</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Trivedi et al. (2022)</a></cite>.
We additionally include <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AmbigQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Min et al. (2020)</a></cite> to assess performance on queries that require disambiguation.
For these datasets, we report Exact Match (EM) and F1 scores as evaluation metrics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we use QA benchmarks on web corpus to validate our practical effectiveness. For Single-hop QA, we use <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MS MARCO</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2016)</a></cite> <span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
                
                
                
              In line with prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Back et al. (2021)</a></cite> that evaluate on sampled subsets of MS MARCO due to the large size of the original benchmark (over 101k queries), we create 500 randomly sampled queries that preserve the original distribution.</span></span></span>.
For challenging reasoning, we use <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CRAG</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a></cite> that covers comprehensive query types. Accuracy (ACC) is reported via LLM-based evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a></cite>, using <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama-3.1-405B</span> as the evaluator.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite>, we directly use the retrieved contents provided by each dataset, with the retrieval context fixed to up to the top-10 contents.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our approach against several strong baselines that serve as bridges between retrieval and generation in question answering tasks.
For reranking baselines, we consider: <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BGE-Reranker-Large</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al. (2023)</a></cite>, a cross-encoder-based pointwise reranker; <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RankZephyr</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pradeep et al. (2023)</a></cite>, a listwise reranker.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For summarization-based baselines,
we consider <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ComPACT</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yoon et al. (2024)</a></cite> and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Refiner</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite>, knowledge-distilled models that generate query-focused abstractive summaries of documents, and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EXIT</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hwang et al. (2024)</a></cite> that prunes irrelevant contexts in the document.
Finally, we consider a unified baseline, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Provence</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chirkova et al. (2025)</a></cite>, which performs both reranking and context summarization in a single step.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results</h3>

<section id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Research Questions</h5>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the effectiveness of our approach, we address the following
research questions:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ 1: Does R2U enhance the performance of the models in QA tasks?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ 2: Does R2U generalize to diverse generators?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">RQ 3: Does each component of R2U contribute to overall effectiveness?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15577v1/crag_analysis.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/279;" width="476" height="279"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Comparison of ACC across various query types in CRAG. The value in parentheses indicates the difference relative to RankZephyr.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>RQ 1: Does R2U enhance the performance of the models in QA tasks?</h3>

<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Overall Performance and True Utility</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> highlight the dominant performance of R2U compared to various baselines in question answering tasks.
We separate the results to focus on different evaluation aspects:
Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> assesses disambiguation or multi-hop reasoning ability on the Wikipedia corpus,
and Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> evaluates performance on the web corpus to validate that our gains in document utility generalize to practical settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in the column named “True Utility” in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, baselines are marked with ✗ if they use a utility function without considering the answer (Eq.<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.1 Motivation: Existing Bridging Models ‣ 2 Proposed Bridging Objective ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) and with ▲ if they incorporate the answer as outcome supervision.
Overall, R2U achieves the highest scores, demonstrating particularly strong performance on disambiguation and multi-hop queries, where deeper document interaction and reasoning are critical.
Compared to baselines, R2U outperforms NaiveRAG by +9.8%pt and the strongest competitor by +8.7%pt in terms of average F1, supporting the hypothesis that aligning document utility with answer generation effectively guides reasoning over multiple sources.
Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> further shows that R2U maintains superior performance on the web corpus, including MS MARCO and CRAG.
This confirms that the benefits of our true utility function extend beyond structured Wikipedia datasets to more diverse, open-domain web content.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Comparison with Distilled Baselines</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While baselines such as RankZephyr <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pradeep et al. (2023)</a></cite> and Refiner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite> also leverage knowledge distillation from large language models for document rewriting, their performance remains lower than that of R2U.
This indicates that the observed gains primarily stem from aligning document utility with answerability, rather than from distillation alone.
Notably, R2U achieves superior performance with a comparatively small 3B module, showing that our approach enables effective distillation even with limited model capacity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Deeper Analysis</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further investigate the role of document utility in complex query resolution, we categorize CRAG queries into four types: aggregation, multi-hop, post-processing-heavy, and others <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a></cite>.
This categorization allows us to disentangle the varying levels of document interaction and reasoning required by different query types. We compare R2U against Naive and RankZephyr, the latter achieving the strongest overall performance among existing baselines on CRAG.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Research Questions ‣ 4.2 Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, R2U attains the highest performance across all query types, with particularly pronounced gains in scenarios demanding higher document utility.
Specifically, R2U outperforms alternatives on aggregation and multi-hop queries, where the effective integration of multiple documents is crucial.
The improvement is even more substantial for post-processing-heavy queries, which require both multi-document reasoning and significant post-processing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These findings highlight that by enhancing the true utility of retrieved documents through the bridge module, R2U enables the generator to more effectively leverage relevant evidence, thereby yielding substantial improvements over competitive baselines.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.15577v1/f1_vs_modelsize_stylized.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/306;" width="476" height="306"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Comparison of average F1 scores across various model sizes using Llama (filled markers) and Qwen (hollow markers).
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>RQ 2: Does R2U generalize to diverse generators?</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address RQ2, we evaluate R2U with various generators, against the best-performing module of each type based on F1 score: RankZephyr for reranking, Refiner for summarization, and Provence for combining both.
We use Llama (<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama-3.2-</span>{<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">1B,3B</span>}<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-Instruct</span>, <span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct</span>) and Qwen (<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Qwen2.5-</span>{<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">0.5B,1B,3B,7B</span>}<span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-Instruct</span>) Family <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2023)</a></cite> as generators.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Deeper Analysis ‣ 4.3 RQ 1: Does R2U enhance the performance of the models in QA tasks? ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> demonstrate the effectiveness and generalizability of our method over different sizes and types of generators.
Notably, Qwen-0.5B with R2U outperforms Llama-1B, and Qwen-1.5B already surpasses larger generators with other methods.
These results highlight that smaller models that lack strong reasoning capabilities often gain more from the effectiveness of R2U.
The full results are available in Table <a href="#A4.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix D Performance of R2U with Different Model Sizes ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>-<a href="#A4.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ Appendix D Performance of R2U with Different Model Sizes ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Performance of R2U with Different Model Sizes ‣ Relevance to Utility: Process-Supervised Rewrite for RAG" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S4.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:432.7pt;height:55.9pt;vertical-align:-25.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-24.0pt,3.1pt) scale(0.9,0.9) ;">
